=== Module 20 - The Predictive Parsing Condition ===

PREDICT(A -> a) = FIRST(a)                        if a is not nullable
               = FIRST(a)\{epsilon} U FOLLOW(A)   if a is nullable
LL(1)  <=>  for every nonterminal, the PREDICT sets of its productions
            are pairwise disjoint.

########## fixtures/expr.grammar

grammar:
  (0) E -> T Ep
  (1) Ep -> + T Ep
  (2) Ep -> epsilon
  (3) T -> F Tp
  (4) Tp -> * F Tp
  (5) Tp -> epsilon
  (6) F -> ( E )
  (7) F -> num
  (8) F -> id
  start: E
  terminals: { (, ), *, +, id, num }

NT     FIRST                      FOLLOW
E      { (, id, num }             { ), $ }
Ep     { +, epsilon }             { ), $ }
T      { (, id, num }             { ), +, $ }
Tp     { *, epsilon }             { ), +, $ }
F      { (, id, num }             { ), *, +, $ }

PREDICT sets:
  (0) E -> T Ep               { (, id, num }
  (1) Ep -> + T Ep            { + }
  (2) Ep -> epsilon           { ), $ }  (nullable: adds FOLLOW)
  (3) T -> F Tp               { (, id, num }
  (4) Tp -> * F Tp            { * }
  (5) Tp -> epsilon           { ), +, $ }  (nullable: adds FOLLOW)
  (6) F -> ( E )              { ( }
  (7) F -> num                { num }
  (8) F -> id                 { id }

VERDICT: LL(1) = YES   every nonterminal's PREDICT sets are disjoint
         -> predictive recursive descent works, no backtracking

########## fixtures/stmts.grammar

grammar:
  (0) P -> L
  (1) L -> S L
  (2) L -> epsilon
  (3) S -> id assign E semi
  (4) E -> id
  (5) E -> num
  start: P
  terminals: { assign, id, num, semi }

NT     FIRST                      FOLLOW
P      { id, epsilon }            { $ }
L      { id, epsilon }            { $ }
S      { id }                     { id, $ }
E      { id, num }                { semi }

PREDICT sets:
  (0) P -> L                  { id, $ }  (nullable: adds FOLLOW)
  (1) L -> S L                { id }
  (2) L -> epsilon            { $ }  (nullable: adds FOLLOW)
  (3) S -> id assign E semi   { id }
  (4) E -> id                 { id }
  (5) E -> num                { num }

VERDICT: LL(1) = YES   every nonterminal's PREDICT sets are disjoint
         -> predictive recursive descent works, no backtracking

########## fixtures/prefix.grammar

grammar:
  (0) S -> a b c
  (1) S -> a b d
  start: S
  terminals: { a, b, c, d }

NT     FIRST                      FOLLOW
S      { a }                      { $ }

PREDICT sets:
  (0) S -> a b c              { a }
  (1) S -> a b d              { a }

VERDICT: LL(1) = NO    1 conflict(s):
  - nonterminal S on token 'a':  [S -> a b c]  vs  [S -> a b d]
  -> the alternatives share a prefix; LEFT-FACTOR them (Module 24):
        S -> a b S'      S' -> c | d

########## fixtures/danglingelse.grammar

grammar:
  (0) S -> if b then S X
  (1) S -> other
  (2) X -> else S
  (3) X -> epsilon
  start: S
  terminals: { b, else, if, other, then }

NT     FIRST                      FOLLOW
S      { if, other }              { else, $ }
X      { else, epsilon }          { else, $ }

PREDICT sets:
  (0) S -> if b then S X      { if }
  (1) S -> other              { other }
  (2) X -> else S             { else }
  (3) X -> epsilon            { else, $ }  (nullable: adds FOLLOW)

VERDICT: LL(1) = NO    1 conflict(s):
  - nonterminal X on token 'else':  [X -> else S]  vs  [X -> epsilon]
  -> classic dangling-else. A predictive parser resolves it by always
     choosing  X -> else S  when the token is 'else' (else binds to the
     nearest if). Modules 25/35 formalise this as a resolved conflict.

